    {
      "path": "_m/9/q/8/y/castro-theatre-wp/castro-theatre-san-francisco.jpg",
      "filename": "Castro Theatre, San Francisco.jpg",
      "caption": "The Castro Theatre in San Francisco.\nThe Castro opened in 1922 and showed first-run films until 1976. It changed hands several times in the following years but continued to operate as a movie theater. In 2001, relatives of the Castro's original owners acquired the theater and restored it. Today, the Castro screens a regular mix of independent and classic movies, including documentaries and art films. Like many theaters of its era, the Castro's interior is an opulent mashup of Spanish, Italian, and Asian design motifs. The opulent exterior marquee and dramatic chandelier in the auditorium were both installed in the 1930s, and the theater can seat more than 1,400 people.",
      "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Castro_Theatre,_San_Francisco.jpg",
      "downloadUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Castro_Theatre%2C_San_Francisco.jpg",
      "artist": "Dllu",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
